# Keep the test suite out of the published sdist. Tests live in the top-level sdk_tests/
# directory, OUTSIDE the agentx_sdk package -- that LOCATION is the real guard: find_packages()
# keeps them out of the wheel, and setuptools' sdist builder does not pull a top-level
# non-package dir into the sdist either. So on the current sdk_tests/ name the prune lines
# below are a no-op; they are kept as defensive belt-and-suspenders, so that a future rename
# to a name setuptools auto-includes (test/ or tests/) cannot silently re-add tests to the
# sdist. The public PyPI + MIT-mirror surface stays shipping-code-only.
prune sdk_tests
recursive-exclude sdk_tests *

# THE EXAMPLES, ON PURPOSE, AND ONLY THE ONES A READER CAN ACTUALLY RUN.
#
# TWO RULES, AND THE SECOND ONE IS A JUDGEMENT. Saying "the rule is keyless" was tidier and
# it was not true: it does not explain three of the nine files that were dropped.
#
# RULE 1, ENFORCED, NOT A TASTE CALL: a published example must run on `pip install
# agentx-security-sdk` plus at most the requirements.txt shipped beside it, a published
# example must NEVER CRASH. It may decline -- say it needs a key, and point at one that does
# not -- but it may not die on an import or a traceback. That property is TESTED by RUNNING
# each published example with the credentials stripped and the packages a reader would not
# have blocked, so nobody has to guess where the line is, and no comment can be graded
# instead of the code.
#
# 🔴 THE RULE USED TO BE "RUNS KEYLESS", AND THAT WAS TOO BLUNT. It dropped 01, the recovery
# demo -- the only published artifact of the thing that differentiates this product. Recovery
# needs the reader's own LLM key; that is what the tier IS, not a defect. What made 01
# unpublishable was that it CRASHED, importing a client the package does not depend on before
# its own friendly message could print. Fixing the crash made it publishable; the rule that
# excluded it was measuring the wrong property.
#
# RULE 2, A CHOICE: of the files that pass rule 1, publish the smallest set that shows
# distinct things -- a block (00), recovery after a block (01), the same block with zero
# configuration (08), and a run where nothing is blocked and the ledger is the point (12).
# More examples is not more value on a page a stranger is skimming.
#
# This replaced an `include examples/*.py` glob that published all thirteen numbered scripts,
# a set nobody chose. Of the nine now unpublished, the reasons differ and are worth keeping
# straight: 02, 09, 10 and 11 drive a gateway the reader does not have; 03 and 07 need an LLM
# client or key and, unlike 01, have not been made to decline gracefully; 04, 05 and 06 pass
# rule 1 outright and were dropped under rule 2 alone -- said plainly, because an unexplained
# drop reads like an oversight and the next person will "fix" it by re-adding them.
#
# A script a reader can open but not run is not an example, it is an advertisement with a
# stack trace at the end. All thirteen still live in the repo; they are simply not shipped.
#
# Scoped to named files rather than a glob, so the ledger and caches an example leaves behind
# when somebody runs it (.agentx.db, __pycache__/, .agentx/) can never be swept into a release,
# AND so that adding a new example to the repo is not the same act as publishing it.
#
# 🔴 THE INCLUDE LIST IS REVIEWED AS PUBLISHED COPY, NOT AS A FILE LIST. The first version was
# a glob and it was wrong three ways at once: `ts-guard/` documents the gateway wire contract
# and cites a private gateway source file and line offset in its comments, which republishes
# the layout of the CLOSED half of this product; it carried a Vitest suite onto a surface this
# file's own first paragraph says is shipping-code-only; and every example imports
# `python-dotenv`, which `install_requires` does not bring, so the scripts were runnable-looking
# and not runnable. An automated check in the test suite asserts all of it against the real
# files, and it found a fourth (an example printing a path inside a private directory) that
# review did not. Naming that check's own path here would be the same defect one sentence
# later, which is exactly what happened the first time this paragraph was written.
#
# ts-guard is deliberately NOT here. It is a demonstration of the gateway's contract, and the
# gateway is the part nobody outside can read.
include examples/00_quickstart_pip.py
include examples/01_self_healing_agent.py
include examples/08_frictionless_agent_protection.py
include examples/12_audit_what_your_agent_did.py
include examples/requirements.txt
include examples/README.md
include examples/mcp/see_block_over_mcp.py
include examples/mcp/mcp.json
include examples/mcp/README.md

global-exclude *.pyc
global-exclude .agentx.db
